body:
The iPhone side of the live motion line (contract #808, recorder-core 0.6.0). Android's is #839.

## What changes

- **Binding:** `PhoneRecorder.trace` calls `rc_phone_trace` and returns it as `MotionTrace` as it is: status, axis, `scale_dps` and the samples. `PhoneRecording` sends it after every tick, and once at Start so "Starting motion sensor" shows at once. A phone without motion sensors never calls it (the core would report `STARTING`, then `NO_SAMPLES`, and read as a fault).
- **Record screen:** elapsed time, stroke rate, the trace, its status line, then the GPS line.
  - The trace is a full-width box, always 48 pt tall. It draws one 2 pt line in primary text blue with round joins, and nothing when the status has nothing to draw.
  - x is `ago_ms` over 10 s, and y is `dps / scale_dps`, inset by half the line's width. One sample alone draws a dot.
  - There's no smoothing and no animation. The trace is hidden from VoiceOver.
  - Until the first status arrives, a phone with motion sensors shows the empty box and "Starting motion sensor", so nothing moves after Start.
- **Status line** (`MotionLine`): the contract's four texts, in the GPS line's style. It is red only for "Couldn’t read the motion sensor. Stroke rate pauses until it’s back."
  - Entering that status and leaving it ("Motion sensor back") are each announced once. It isn't a live region otherwise.
  - The motion line and the GPS line now sit together, 6 pt apart, so Hide fits better on an iPhone SE. With the two-line warning, Hide's label is on screen there, and its bottom few points sit under the bar until you scroll.
- **#840** (the ±5 deg/s floor) needs no app change. The app draws the core's own `scale_dps`, and a UI test checks that a still phone draws almost flat.
- The core's stroke lines (`RC_STROKE`) now reach the sink as `.stroke` so the fixture runner can check them. `RecordingFiles` doesn't write them: the server rebuilds stroke rate from the two files.

## Tests

- **Fixture runner** (`FixtureReplayTests`):
  - It takes `phone_*` and `trace_phone_*`. Each case must write `track.csv`, `motion.csv` and `stroke.csv` byte for byte, plus `trace.csv` for a trace case.
  - It checks `expect_session`.
  - It fails by name if `trace_phone_flat` or `trace_phone_paddle` is missing.
  - I proved it can fail: a wrong status mapping failed `trace.csv`, and a changed `expect_session` range failed the stroke rate check.
- **Unit tests:**
  - the words for each status, and that only `NO_SAMPLES` is red;
  - the two announcements, and that nothing else is said;
  - where the samples go in the box;
  - that the model announces once;
  - that stroke lines are never written to the files;
  - that every fixture trace block reads back as written.
- **UI tests** (`MotionTraceTests`, `MotionTraceLargeTextTests`). The simulator has no motion sensors, so with `-SampleServer` the app shows a block of the core's own fixtures, passed in `PADDLESNITCH_MOTION_TRACE`. The tests cover:
  - each status's words;
  - a line drawn only for still and moving, almost flat when still;
  - the 48 pt box in every status, and that the GPS line doesn't move between one-line statuses;
  - red only for the error;
  - the order on screen;
  - the accessibility audit;
  - the largest text size.
  - `RecordTests` now also checks there's no motion line without motion sensors.
- **Runs:** the motion and record UI tests pass on the newest iOS simulator. The motion tests also pass on an **iPhone SE (3rd generation), iOS 26.5**, run by hand because CI runs only a standard iPhone. Screenshots of each status on both phones were reviewed.
- `make lint` is clean, and every package test passes.

## Reviews

- **tester:** the tests cover the contract and lead's runner requirement. Changes from its points:
  - added a check that a phone without motion sensors shows no motion line;
  - renamed a test whose name claimed more than it checked;
  - wrote in the lessons that the SE run is by hand.

  Left as they are:
  - no test that "Starting motion sensor" shows at once (it needs Core Motion injected; the placeholder below now covers the screen);
  - the no-jump check skips `NO_SAMPLES`, whose two-line warning moves the GPS line as expected.
- **design-usability:** the order, drawing, states, VoiceOver, Reduce Motion and tokens match the contract. Changes from its points:
  - the empty box and "Starting motion sensor" show from the first frame;
  - the GPS line is grouped with the motion line, 6 pt apart;
  - both status lines get the same modifiers;
  - the line is inset left and right too;
  - one sample draws a dot.

  Left as they are:
  - Android's exact 16/4 dp spacing, which the contract doesn't set.
  - **Raised for later, not changed here:** at the largest text size the hold-to-stop bar covers much of an SE screen. It keeps room for the hidden "Keep holding to stop." on every tap. This is older layout, and the tests now scroll above it.
- **copy-style:** the five strings match the contract character for character, curly apostrophes included. Four wording fixes in the README, CLAUDE.md and comments are made.
- **security-privacy:** not run. Nothing touches accounts, the network or personal data. The only new switch, `PADDLESNITCH_MOTION_TRACE`, works only with `-SampleServer`.

