## Description

Fixes #16801.

In DATABASE mode, a `@Transactional` method of a class annotated with `@CurrentTenant` failed with a `TenantException` when called inside `Tenants.withId` while the tenant resolver resolves another tenant, as one that resolves the tenant from the web request does outside a request:

```groovy
@CurrentTenant
@Transactional
class WidgetService {

    void save(String name) {
        new Widget(name: name).save(flush: true)
    }
}

Tenants.withId('test') {
    widgetService.save('widget')
}
// TenantException: Cannot resolve a single current tenant: [default, test] are bound on this thread for different datastores. ...
```

These are two bugs from #16066, and a third with the same cause.

### `Tenants` and `@CurrentTenant` inside `Tenants.withId`

`Tenants.withId(tenantId)` binds the tenant for the datastore of the default connection. Inside it, `Tenants.currentId()` and the other methods of `Tenants` that take no datastore, and a `@CurrentTenant` class that is not a data service, looked their datastore up through `GormApiResolver.findSingleDatastore()`. With more than one connection that resolved the current tenant, so in DATABASE mode it returned the datastore of the tenant's connection. No tenant is bound for that datastore, so they took the tenant the resolver resolves:

```groovy
// DATABASE mode, a tenant resolver that resolves 'two'
Tenants.withId('one') {
    Tenants.currentId() // 'two'
}
```

A `@CurrentTenant` method then bound that tenant for the datastore of `one`. The thread held two tenants, and a `@Transactional` method failed as above. With a resolver that resolves no tenant, the `@CurrentTenant` method failed with a `TenantNotFoundException`, and with a tenant the datastore has no connection for, with a `ConfigurationException`.

`findSingleDatastore()` now returns the datastore of the default connection whenever one is registered, as on 7.x, where it returned the single datastore registered by type. Its callers all want that datastore: `Tenants`, `@CurrentTenant`, the fallback of `findServiceDatastore`, and the generated `getTargetDatastore()`, which calls `getDatastoreForConnection` on it. A `@CurrentTenant` data service was not affected, as `@CurrentTenant` looks its datastore up through `findServiceDatastore`.

### `Tenants.withId` with a domain class

`Tenants.withId(domainClass, tenantId)` looked the datastore up through `findDatastore(domainClass)`, which in DATABASE mode resolves the current tenant as well, the one bound or the one the resolver resolves. It bound the given tenant for the datastore of that tenant, and failed:

```groovy
// DATABASE mode, a tenant resolver that resolves 'two'
Tenants.withId(Team, 'one') {
    new Team(name: 'Wolves').save(flush: true)
}
// ConfigurationException: No datastore found for connection named [one]
```

It now binds the tenant for the datastore the domain class is mapped to, looked up through `findServiceDatastore`, as `Tenants.withTenant(domainClass, tenantId)` does too.

### The transaction manager of a tenant

The `getTransactionManager()` that `@Transactional` and data services generate looked up the tenant with `CurrentTenantHolder.get()`, which throws a `TenantException` when different tenants are bound for different datastores. That is what the spec in the issue shows. It now calls `GormRegistry.findCurrentTenantTransactionManager`, which looks up the tenant bound for the default datastore, or for the datastore of the data service, and returns the transaction manager of the connection of that name, as before.

The lookup moved from the generated code into `GormRegistry`. Classes compiled against 8.0.0 keep the unscoped lookup until they are recompiled. The change to `findSingleDatastore()` applies to them without that, and covers the case in the issue.

### Tests

- `CurrentTenantTransformSpec` (`grails-datamapping-core-test`) has five new features, in DATABASE mode on GORM's in-memory datastore: for `Tenants.currentId()` and a `@CurrentTenant` method inside `Tenants.withId` while the resolver resolves another tenant, for a `@Transactional` method while another datastore has a different tenant, and for `Tenants.withId` with a domain class, at the top level and inside `Tenants.withId`. All fail without the change.
- `DatabaseMultiTenantCurrentTenantTransactionalSpec` (`grails-data-hibernate5-core` and `grails-data-hibernate7-core`, new) covers the case in the issue on GORM for Hibernate 5 and 7, with a `@CurrentTenant` `@Transactional` class and a resolver that resolves `default`. Both features fail without the change, in both modules.
- `GormApiResolverSpec` has two new features for `findSingleDatastore()`, with a tenant bound for the default datastore and with a session of another connection bound. Both fail without the change. The feature `findSingleDatastore delegates to findDatastore when more than one qualifier is registered` now says what it checks.
- `GormRegistrySpec` has four features for `findCurrentTenantTransactionManager`.
- `TransactionalTransformSpec` and `ServiceTransformSpec` have a new feature each for the generated `getTransactionManager()` while another datastore has a different tenant. Both fail without the change. In the one of `ServiceTransformSpec`, the domain class of the data service is mapped to a datastore other than the default one, so it also fails when the getter looks up the tenant bound for the default datastore.

These pass with the change:

- all tests of `grails-datamapping-core`, `grails-datamapping-core-test`, `grails-data-simple`, `grails-datastore-web`, `grails-data-hibernate5-core`, `grails-data-hibernate7-core`, `grails-data-mongodb-core` and `grails-data-neo4j-core`;
- `codeStyle` for `grails-datamapping-core`, `grails-data-hibernate5-core` and `grails-data-hibernate7-core`.

**Tier 1** (`tools/ui-shot.sh`). The rest is docs and skills.

## Why
Building every UI mode (#48) took a lot of rediscovery: which Rust arms a mode needs, how the layout builders fit together, where real reference images can be found, and several capture pitfalls that produced misleading screenshots. This writes that down so the next mode or fix starts from it.

## Changes
- **`.claude/skills/ui-mode/SKILL.md`** (new). It covers:
  - what a mode is made of and every Rust arm to add;
  - the `Layout` patterns;
  - the start page (`home.css`) rules, including how to avoid an oracle change;
  - icon palette clashes between modes;
  - the fidelity loop: reference sources, distrusting recollection and later OS themes, capture, independent review, and recording the result in `modes.md`;
  - how to batch several modes with subagents.
- **`tools/ui-shot.sh`** (new). `tools/ui-shot.sh <mode> [--url=URL] [X,Y ...]` screenshots the debug build in a mode under Xvfb, once after load and once after each click, into `target/ui-shots/`. It handles the pitfalls hit in #48:
  - it restarts a dead Xvfb and fails on a blank capture;
  - it parks the pointer off the window, so no tooltip lands in the shot;
  - it waits for the main window rather than a helper window;
  - it kills Lantern by PID.
- **Steward skill:**
  - `pipefail` when piping checks;
  - pointing the maintainer at the waiting run for the oracle approval;
  - designing around existing assertions, and checking `git status` after `--update-snapshots`;
  - naming the tested commit in the PR description and checking mergeability before calling a PR done.
- `CLAUDE.md` and `docs/architecture.md` list the new skill and script.

## Testing
- `shellcheck tools/*.sh`: clean.
- I ran `tools/ui-shot.sh tv-1997 760,580` against the debug build. It wrote the loaded page and the open full-screen Options panel at 800×600, with no tooltip and no blank capture.
- No product code changed, so I didn't re-run the unit, web or smoke tests.
- `/code-review`: no findings.

